  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/identity",
    path: "packages/identity",
    role: "Identity layer shared across the cloud surface, accounts, and mobile pairing.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/httpapi-codegen",
    path: "packages/httpapi-codegen",
    role: "Code generator for the typed HttpApi route groups consumed by the SDK.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/bench-tui",
    path: "packages/bench-tui",
    role: "Standalone test interface and benchmark dashboard for Bun packages — SolidJS over OpenTUI.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/simulation",
    path: "packages/simulation",
    role: "Driver + recording + protocol layer for the simulation harness used by browser, terminal and computer engines.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/script",
    path: "packages/script",
    role: "Cross-package CLI scripts and ops tooling — includes the Railway deploy script referenced from the mobile doc.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/nikcli-island",
    path: "packages/nikcli-island",
    role: "Swift package that ships NikcliIsland.app, the macOS notch HUD for live sessions. Outside the JS build graph.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/containers",
    path: "packages/containers",
    role: "Prebuilt GitHub Actions container images: base, bun-node, rust, tauri-linux, publish.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/extensions/zed",
    path: "packages/extensions/zed",
    role: "Zed editor extension that wires nikcli as an agent server.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/console-app",
    path: "packages/console/app",
    role: "Console-suite Vite app shell for the hosted admin surface.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/console-core",
    path: "packages/console/core",
    role: "Console-suite shared core: AWS SDK helpers, SST infra glue, and JSX-email rendering.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/console-function",
    path: "packages/console/function",
    role: "Console-suite Cloudflare Worker functions exposed under the console surface.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/console-mail",
    path: "packages/console/mail",
    role: "Console-suite transactional-email worker built on @jsx-email.",
  },
  {
    name: "@nikcli-ai/console-resource",
    path: "packages/console/resource",
    role: "Console-suite Cloudflare resource bindings (KV, R2, Durable Objects, etc.).",
  },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Packages & Suite"
  subtitle="nikcli is a monorepo. packages/nikcli is the core CLI/TUI/server; the packages below provide supporting infrastructure, provider integrations, and surface-area extensions."
  toc={toc}
  description="Overview of every package in the nikcli monorepo, with detailed documentation for @nikcli-ai/llm and @nikcli-ai/http-recorder."
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The nikcli monorepo lives under <code>packages/</code>. The base product is
      <code>packages/nikcli</code> — the CLI binary, TUI, agent runtime, session storage, HTTP
      server, and built-in tool registry. Every other package is a supporting layer: provider
      infrastructure (<code>llm</code>), test tooling (<code>http-recorder</code>), SDKs, native
      surfaces (desktop, mobile), and hosted services (cloud, inference, slack).
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Dependency direction">
      <code>packages/nikcli</code> depends on <code>@nikcli-ai/llm</code> for all LLM traffic.
      <code>@nikcli-ai/llm</code>'s test suite depends on <code>@nikcli-ai/http-recorder</code>
      to replay provider responses without live network calls. Neither recorder nor llm depends
      back on <code>packages/nikcli</code>.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="llm" class="doc-section">
    <h2>@nikcli-ai/llm</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/llm</code> is the multi-provider LLM client library that handles every
      inference request nikcli makes. It is built on
      <a href="https://effect.website" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Effect</a> and the
      <a href="https://sdk.vercel.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel AI SDK</a> (<code>ai</code>
      package) for streaming, and exposes a uniform <code>LLMClient</code> service regardless of
      which backend is in use.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Route system" eyebrow="packages/llm/src/route">
        <p>
          A <em>Route</em> composes four orthogonal axes — Protocol, Endpoint, Auth, and
          Framing — into a typed, registerable unit. <code>LLMClient</code> resolves the
          correct route from a <code>ModelRef</code> at call time.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Providers" eyebrow="packages/llm/src/providers">
        <p>
          Named provider modules for Anthropic, OpenAI, Azure, Amazon Bedrock, Google Gemini,
          GitHub Copilot, Cloudflare, OpenRouter, xAI, and the built-in
          <code>nikcli-inference</code> gateway. Each exports a typed <code>model()</code>
          factory.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Protocols" eyebrow="packages/llm/src/protocols">
        <p>
          Wire-protocol adapters that own body construction, schema validation, and stream
          parsing: <code>anthropic-messages</code>, <code>openai-chat</code>,
          <code>openai-responses</code>, <code>bedrock-converse</code>, and
          <code>gemini</code>.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Tool runtime" eyebrow="packages/llm/src/tool-runtime.ts">
        <p>
          Agentic tool-use loop built on top of <code>LLMClient.stream</code>. Executes
          tool calls, accumulates results, and re-submits until a stop condition is met or
          the step limit is reached.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>

    <h3>How nikcli consumes @nikcli-ai/llm</h3>
    <p>
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/provider/llm-client.ts</code> creates and exports the Effect
      <code>Layer</code> for <code>LLMClient</code>:
    </p>
    <pre
      class="not-prose overflow-x-auto rounded-[var(--radius-md)] border border-terminal-border/60 bg-terminal-code px-5 py-4 text-sm font-mono leading-relaxed text-terminal-text"
    ><code
>// packages/nikcli/src/provider/llm-client.ts
import &#123; LLMClient, RequestExecutor &#125; from "@nikcli-ai/llm/route"

export const defaultLayer = Layer.suspend(() =>
  LLMClient.layer.pipe(Layer.provide(RequestExecutor.defaultLayer))
)</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4">
      The session LLM pipeline in <code>src/session/llm/</code> and the provider registry in
      <code>src/provider/provider.ts</code> then use <code>LLMClient.stream</code> /
      <code>LLMClient.generate</code> to drive all model calls, and import provider model
      factories (e.g. <code>Anthropic.model()</code>, <code>OpenAI.model()</code>) from
      <code>@nikcli-ai/llm/providers</code> to build typed <code>ModelRef</code> values
      from user config.
    </p>

    <h3>Key exports</h3>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Export path</th>
          <th>What it provides</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>LLMClient</code>, <code>LLMCore</code>, schema types (<code>LLMRequest</code>,
            <code>LLMResponse</code>, <code>ModelRef</code>, etc.), <code>Tool</code> helpers,
            <code>Runtime</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm/route</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>LLMClient</code> service + Effect layer, <code>Route.make</code>,
            <code>RequestExecutor</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm/providers</code></td>
          <td>
            All named provider modules re-exported; individual providers also available at
            <code>@nikcli-ai/llm/providers/anthropic</code>, <code>.../openai</code>, etc.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm/protocols/&lt;name&gt;</code></td>
          <td>
            Individual protocol adapters: <code>anthropic-messages</code>,
            <code>openai-chat</code>, <code>openai-responses</code>,
            <code>bedrock-converse</code>, <code>gemini</code>,
            <code>openai-compatible-chat</code>
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm/core</code></td>
          <td>
            Thin wrapper around the Vercel AI SDK <code>streamText</code> for cases that
            bypass the route system
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>@nikcli-ai/llm/provider</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>Provider.make</code> — typed factory for declaring a new provider
            definition
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="http-recorder" class="doc-section">
    <h2>@nikcli-ai/http-recorder</h2>
    <p>
      <code>packages/http-recorder</code> is a record-and-replay HTTP fixture library written
      with Effect. It intercepts <code>HttpClient</code> calls, serialises request/response pairs
      into versioned JSON <em>cassettes</em>, and plays them back deterministically in tests —
      eliminating live network calls while keeping provider protocol coverage.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also handles WebSocket interactions: a cassette can contain both
      <code>http</code> and <code>websocket</code> interaction records in the same file.
    </p>

    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Cassette storage" eyebrow="packages/http-recorder/src/cassette.ts">
        <p>
          Effect <code>Service</code> and <code>Layer</code> for reading, writing, appending,
          and listing cassette files under a configurable directory. Cassettes are
          <code>version: 1</code> JSON files containing an array of typed interactions.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Record / replay layer" eyebrow="packages/http-recorder/src/effect.ts">
        <p>
          <code>cassetteLayer(name, options)</code> returns an Effect <code>Layer</code> that
          replaces <code>HttpClient</code>. In <code>record</code> mode it proxies the real
          network and writes cassettes; in <code>replay</code> mode it matches recorded
          interactions and returns the saved response.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Secret redaction" eyebrow="packages/http-recorder/src/redaction.ts">
        <p>
          Scans cassettes for patterns that resemble API keys, auth tokens, or other secrets
          before writing. If findings are detected the write is refused and the caller receives
          a descriptive error.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Matching & diff" eyebrow="packages/http-recorder/src/matching.ts">
        <p>
          Configurable request-matching strategy (default: URL + method + body hash). A
          <code>sequential</code> dispatch mode replays interactions in order. Mismatch errors
          include a human-readable diff of expected vs. actual request fields.
        </p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>

    <h3>Modes</h3>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Mode</th>
          <th>Behaviour</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>replay</code> (default)</td>
          <td>
            Loads the cassette from disk and returns the saved response for each matched
            request. Fails with a clear error if the cassette is missing or no interaction
            matches.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>record</code></td>
          <td>
            Forwards requests to the real upstream, captures responses, and appends them to
            the cassette. Blocked if the response body contains detected secrets.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>passthrough</code></td>
          <td>
            Skips cassette logic entirely and forwards all requests to the real network.
            Useful for one-off exploration without recording.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Usage in @nikcli-ai/llm tests</h3>
    <p>
      The llm package's test helpers (<code>packages/llm/test/recorded-test.ts</code>,
      <code>recorded-websocket.ts</code>) import <code>HttpRecorder</code> from
      <code>@nikcli-ai/http-recorder</code> and wrap each provider test case in a
      <code>cassetteLayer</code> pointing at
      <code>packages/llm/test/fixtures/recordings/</code>. Running with
      <code>RECORD=true</code> refreshes the fixtures against live providers; the default CI
      run replays them.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Setting up recording">
      <code>packages/llm</code> ships a helper script:
      <code>bun run setup:recording-env</code> (<code>script/setup-recording-env.ts</code>)
      that prepopulates the required provider API keys before a record pass.
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="package-table" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Full Package Table</h2>
    <p>
      Packages without a <code>package.json</code> at their root (e.g.
      <code>packages/sdk</code> which nests <code>packages/sdk/js</code>, or
      <code>packages/extensions/zed</code> which is a Zed extension manifest) are listed by
      their published package name. The <code>packages/console/</code> tree publishes five
      sibling packages under the <code>@nikcli-ai/console-*</code> scope; the
      <code>packages/containers/</code> tree ships prebuilt Docker images rather than a
      single npm package.
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="The three automation engines">
      <code>@nikcli-ai/computer-use</code>, <code>@nikcli-ai/browser-control</code>, and
      <code>@nikcli-ai/terminal-control</code> are sibling engines that share the same CLI
      grammar (<code>start / info / recording / bundle</code>) and the same per-workspace
      background daemon pattern. Their <code>bundle</code> commands all produce the same
      artifact set (<code>screenshot.png</code>, <code>demo.mp4</code>,
      <code>preview.gif</code>, <code>manifest.json</code>, <code>pr.md</code>) so PR
      pipelines can treat them uniformly.
    </DocCallout>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Package</th>
          <th>Path</th>
          <th>Role</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {packages.map((pkg) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{pkg.name}</code></td>
            <td><code>{pkg.path}</code></td>
            <td>{pkg.role}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
